<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    The constant think trees (system 9: AI — interrupts). RimWorld: RaceProperties.thinkTreeConstant, the
    second tree every pawn carries alongside its main one.

    The difference between this file and ThinkTrees_Humanlike.xml / ThinkTrees_Animal.xml is *when* it is
    consulted, not what it can say. A main tree is only ever asked when a pawn has nothing to do, so until
    this file existed the entire AI layer was unreachable for a pawn already doing something: a citizen
    hauling a rock kept hauling while a raider shot at them, and a sleeping one slept through it. This tree
    runs every ConstantThinkTreeTuning.IntervalTicks ticks whether or not a job is running, and its answer
    ends the job that is.

    Its own file, not a third ThinkTreeDef appended to either existing one: DefLoader.AddDirectory reads
    every XML file under a Def folder, so an additive Def never needs an edit to a shared file (CLAUDE.md) —
    and both existing tree files are ones other lanes are actively adding tiers to.

    WHAT IS IN HERE, AND WHAT DELIBERATELY IS NOT. Only the combat tier. That is not a stub: it is the answer
    to "a pawn should not abandon surgery because someone walked past". A tier that contributes no job
    interrupts nothing, so every non-threat is handled by simply not being in this tree — no priority
    numbers, no per-job exception lists. RimWorld's own humanlike constant tree also carries a self-
    extinguish tier for a burning pawn; this port has fire (Things/Fire.cs) but no pawn-attached fire hediff
    for one to extinguish, so there is nothing here to route to and it is recorded as absent rather than
    invented.
  -->

  <ThinkTreeDef>
    <defName>HumanlikeConstant</defName>
    <thinkRoot Class="SimWorld.AI.ThinkNode_Priority">
      <subNodes>

        <!--
          The gate: may what this pawn is doing right now be thrown away? Everything that can interrupt sits
          under it, so the answer is asked once rather than per tier. See the node's own doc for the three
          rules it encodes — the short version is that a job marked casualInterruptible="false" and a job the
          player forced both survive this tree, and both can still be ended by the damage path
          (Pawn_JobTracker.Notify_DamageTaken), which does not consult this gate at all. That split is the
          whole design: seeing a threat is a weaker claim on a pawn's attention than being hit by one.
        -->
        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.ThinkNode_ConditionalCanDoConstantThinkTreeJobNow">
          <subNodes>
            <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_AIFightEnemies" />
          </subNodes>
        </li>

      </subNodes>
    </thinkRoot>
  </ThinkTreeDef>

  <ThinkTreeDef>
    <defName>AnimalConstant</defName>
    <thinkRoot Class="SimWorld.AI.ThinkNode_Priority">
      <subNodes>

        <li Class="SimWorld.AI.ThinkNode_ConditionalCanDoConstantThinkTreeJobNow">
          <subNodes>

            <!--
              A grudge outranks a faction quarrel here exactly as it does in the animal main tree: an animal
              that has been speared mid-wander should break off and come for the hunter, and JobGiver_Manhunter
              already knows which pawn that is. Falls through when the grudge has expired or its target is
              gone, so a tamed animal still defends its faction on the tier below.
            -->
            <li Class="SimWorld.AI.ThinkNode_ConditionalAngryAtHandler">
              <subNodes>
                <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_Manhunter" />
              </subNodes>
            </li>

            <li Class="SimWorld.AI.JobGiver_AIFightEnemies" />

          </subNodes>
        </li>

      </subNodes>
    </thinkRoot>
  </ThinkTreeDef>

</Defs>
